WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:03.789
I want to talk about racism today, but I'm
already pretty nervous, because, one, I am

00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:09.380
definitely not an expert and, two, racism
is big and complex and nuanced and the Internet

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.340
-- god bless it -- doesn't really specialize
in nuance.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.090
But a bunch of recent surveys have shown that
about 75% of white Americans do not think

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:21.310
that there is racial bias in the criminal
justice system, and a slight majority of white

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.950
Americans don't think that racism is a significant
problem in America.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:29.520
But so far as I can tell, Hank, whether systemic
bias against African-Americans exists in the

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.370
United States is not really a debatable point,
so I want to look today at some data.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.079
OK, let's begin with the criminal justice
system.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:41.059
So last year, the US Sentencing Commission
released a report showing that African American

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:46.430
men's prison sentences were, on average, 20%
longer than white men's prison's sentences

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.499
when they were convicted of similar crimes.

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:52.000
And in fact, in the past decade, the racial
gap in sentencing has been widening.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.389
Also, black people and white people use illegal
drugs at almost identical rates, but black

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:59.589
people are three times more likely to be arrested
for drug possession.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:03.190
Also, African Americans are far more likely
to be stopped and searched by police, at least

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.920
in the jurisdictions that keep good records,
even though, in many cases, the contraband

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.930
hit rate -- the chance that an officer will
find something actually illegal -- is higher

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:11.930
among white people.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.619
By the way, there's links about all this stuff
in the dooblydoo.

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:16.969
But there's just so much, Hank, I mean, people
convicted of crimes and then later exonerated

00:01:16.969 --> 00:01:19.290
by DNA evidence are disproportionately black.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.580
Black kids are more likely to be tried as
adults than white kids and more likely to

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.180
be sentenced to life in prison, and even after
release, black former inmates are less likely

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.880
to get callbacks from potential employers
than white former inmates, regardless of the

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:30.880
crime committed.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.780
Speaking of which, let's turn to the job market.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.520
So one of the things that makes it so hard
to isolate race when trying to study it is

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.840
that so many other factors are at work in
systemic injustice, right?

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.719
Like, there's class and health and wealth,
none of which are fully separable from race.

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:48.149
But -- OK, so a large 2004 University of Chicago
study submitted thousands of resumes to a

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:54.909
huge variety of employers, and all the resumes
were completely identical -- except for the

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:55.909
applicant's name.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:02.289
It could be Emily or Brendan or Lakisha or
Jamal, and Lakisha and Jamal got 50% fewer

00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:06.719
callbacks than Emily and Brendan, despite
having literally identical resumes.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:08.930
Hank, I'm pretty sure that's about race.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:12.710
In fact, studies consistently show racial
bias in employment and hiring in the US, and

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.780
also around the world, but I only have four
minutes, so links in the dooblydoo!

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.920
In education, again, the evidence of systemic
bias is pretty overwhelming.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.140
For instance, among American high schools
with mostly black and Latino students, only

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.800
74% offer Algebra II as a class; just 66%
offer chemistry...

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.200
the percentages for mostly white schools are
much higher.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.650
When it comes to healthcare, you could write
a book about racial bias, and in fact, people

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.430
have, but I'll just quote from an American
College of Physicians report from 2010:

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.230
"Overwhelming evidence shows that racial and
ethnic minorities are prone to poorer-quality

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.620
healthcare than white Americans, even when
factors such as insurance status are controlled."

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.040
And as the reports point out, by some measures,
including life expectancy, which is really

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.480
the ultimate measure, the disparity has been
increasing for decades.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.200
Now, Hank, I want to be clear that most of
this research establishes correlations, which

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.420
isn't the same thing as causation.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.380
Like, certainly the tremendous economic inequality
right now in the United States is a factor

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.380
in racial disparities.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.590
But then of course, race also factors into
class and economic status.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.380
Like, for instance, much of the racial wealth
disparity in the US is due to inheritance:

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.590
white people are far more likely to inherit
money and land than black people are, and

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.110
that's due in large part to the fact that
for almost all of American history, it was

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.340
basically impossible for African Americans
to accrue wealth.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:24.990
Now, Hank, I'm obviously just scratching the
surface here, but to deny the existence of

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:30.680
systemic racism is to deny a huge body of
evidence indicating that racial bias affects

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.850
almost every facet of American life.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.100
Hank, the last thing I want to say is that,
while I think statistics and data are really

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.111
important, I also think it's important to
listen to the voices of the people who have

00:03:40.111 --> 00:03:41.620
been affected by racism.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.480
Data is cold in a way that humans are not,
and to really understand these statistics,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.070
and their impact on the real lives of real
people, we need to find ways to listen to

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.070
those people.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.630
Hank, I've put together a playlist and some
links in the dooblydoo as a start.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:55.360
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

